{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>Put, I pray, your hand under my thigh.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): he asked of him two things — the first, that he make a covenant with him to serve him with the service of a servant to a master, אומנג\"י in the vernacular; and the second, that he swear to him by YHWH, the God of the heavens and the God of the earth." "<b>And know</b> that the custom in those days was to place one's hand under the thigh of him who is under one's authority. And so R. Elazar, of blessed memory, explained: שים נא ידך תחת ירכי — it was their custom, for the master would sit and his thigh would be over the servant's hand, in the sense of: behold, my hand is under your authority to do your will; and this practice still exists today in the land of India." "<b>And by way of the Midrash:</b> שים נא ידך תחת ירכי ואשביעך וגו' — it is all one matter, as our Rabbis, of blessed memory, said: he bound him by the commandment of circumcision, for it was the commandment newly given to him. And the reason he mentioned 'by YHWH, the God of the heavens' is that the entire Torah consists of the names of the Holy One, blessed be He, and one commandment from among the commandments is surely one portion of the names. And understand this." "<b>And the God of the earth.</b> Do not be surprised that Abraham mentioned 'the God of the earth,' for he was in the land of Canaan when he bound the servant by oath. And the Scriptures always attribute divinity to a particular nation and to a particular land — as it is written: 'the God of Israel,' 'the God of Jerusalem,' as it says (II Chronicles 32): ״וידברו אל אלהי ירושלים כעל אלהי עמי הארץ״. And we do not find in all the Scriptures that the Name, blessed be He, is attributed to the other nations or to the other lands, because the Holy Land is the inheritance of YHWH in particular. Therefore Abraham mentioned there 'the God of the earth,' for there he was; and had he been outside the Land, he would not have mentioned this. Therefore he said immediately after: 'YHWH, the God of the heavens, who took me from my father's house' — and he did not mention 'the God of the earth,' because he mentioned his father's house and the land of his birth, which are outside the Land and were idolaters. Therefore he did not mention it thus, for Abraham did not wish to set his word upon any but Elohim Most High — not upon the god of outside the Land. And this is their saying, of blessed memory: 'Whoever dwells outside the Land is like one who has no God,' as it is said (I Samuel 26): ״כי גרשוני היום מהסתפח בנחלת ה׳ לאמר לך עבוד אלהים אחרים״." "<b>And the explanation</b> of this statement is: he is like one who is not of the particular nation, and who does not have that particular God designated over it — who is the Master of all, blessed be He." "<b>And the wise.</b> R. E. explained that the reason Scripture mentions 'the earth' is that all marriages are from heaven, in the manner that the Sages, of blessed memory, said: 'the daughter of so-and-so for so-and-so,' even from across the sea."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :register {:flags {0 ["God"], 2 ["God"], 3 ["God"], 4 ["God"]}}, :notes {0 "The Romance vernacular term אומנג\"י (homage) is left in transliteration as Rabbeinu Bahya cites it.", 1 "The epithet ר\"א is resolved as R. Elazar, following the common reference in rabbinic commentaries to R. Elazar of Worms or another R. Elazar; resolution uncertain.", 5 "ר\"E expanded as ר\"א in the source, resolved as Rabbi Eli'ezer per the context of classical exegetes cited by Rabbeinu Bahya."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:14:41.870545671Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}